  desc: 'Six small clouds drift across the sky, edges drawn in colored light, each one a different voice — warm plucks, loping keys, deep pulse, bell showers, long violet chimes, a rose-glass shimmer. Your hands are two breaths of light drifting through the dark — soft, wide, barely there — and weather simply RISES where a breath meets a cloud: the nearer, the louder. The clouds do not care about you; they wander their own sky, and you move to meet them. That indifference is what makes finding one feel like meeting an animal. Stay with one and the weather slowly deepens — drawn edges burning brighter, pulses subdividing, the voice climbing an octave — and it KEEPS PLAYING; intensity is something you ride, not a bar you fill. Hold a cloud at full storm and you are BUILDING: hats creep in beneath the rumble, the cloud stutters white on the beat — and on the next bar it DROPS: the real forked lightning, a flood, its thunder, and then the beat arrives. Kick and hats roll for eight bars, earned by your build, the whole sky glowing on every kick, before the weather breathes back down. Charge another cloud mid-groove and drop again. Where lightning soaks the ground twice, a stalked jellyfish blooms and sings in the drone — then, minutes later, folds away again. Nothing is kept. Everything can be grown again. That is the point.',
  interact: 'L is a warm breath of light that rests at the LEFT edge and travels the whole sky as you reach — all the way to the far right; R mirrors from the right edge. Either hand can play any cloud, and crossing your hands crosses the breaths. There is nothing to aim: clouds near your breath simply play LOUDER, so sweeping is mixing — and PLACEMENT is touch: the heart of a cloud is loud, its edges soft. Charging only counts near the heart, and a charging cloud grows restless, so the drop is earned by FOLLOWING the storm. Two builds at once just works — two drops, one beat, more fun. Staying with a cloud slowly deepens its weather — subdivisions, brightness, an octave — and the music never stops for it. Holding at full storm is the BUILD (hats creeping in); the lightning is the DROP, landing on the bar; the beats play after the drop for eight bars, then fade back into rain. The clouds drift indifferent — you go to them. Pass a breath through a grown jellyfish to strum it. Two players, one hand each, works beautifully.',
